;; chess.sks
;; Motoi Cart — Flagship (SD framebuffer)
;;
;; (cart
;;   :name            "chess"
;;   :category        :game
;;   :sub-category    :board
;;   :audience        :kid
;;   :complexity      5
;;   :runtime-tier    :runs-on-core
;;   :render          :sd-framebuffer
;;   :one-line        "Play through the ten greatest chess games ever played — pick a side, and Motoi plays the other, either faithfully replaying the historical line or trusting its own brain when it sees a better move — a real 8x8 board drawn on the 320x240 screen."
;;   :verbs-used      ("set-mode" "clear" "set-color" "rect-fill" "rect" "disc"
;;                     "fb/text" "fb/dump" "make-vector" "vector-ref" "vector-set!"
;;                     "vector-length" "quotient" "remainder" "modulo" "abs" "min"
;;                     "max" "number->string" "string-append" "eq?" "cons" "car"
;;                     "cdr" "list" "length" "reverse" "for-each" "when" "cond"
;;                     "case" "let" "let*" "set!")
;;   :inspiration     "Chess — a thousand years of a game with no dice and nothing hidden, and the machine dream since Turing hand-simulated 'Turochamp' in 1948 of a program that could pick its own move.")
;;
;; HOW TO RUN:
;;   motoi run carts/chess.sks
;;
;; WHAT THIS IS
;;   Chess on Motoi's real SD screen (320x240). The board is one vector of 64
;;   squares — index 0 is a8, index 63 is h1 — and each square holds a small
;;   number: 0 is empty, +1..+6 are the white pieces (pawn..king) and -1..-6 the
;;   black ones. Numbers (not symbols) so decoding a piece is plain arithmetic;
;;   a vector so reading a square is instant — both matter, because to pick a
;;   move the machine has to look at the board many, many times.
;;
;;   Every rule of movement is here and correct: pawns step one (or two from
;;   home), capture on the diagonals, and turn into a queen at the far rank;
;;   knights jump their L; bishops, rooks and queens slide until something
;;   blocks them; the king steps one square any direction. The program will
;;   never make an illegal move, and it will never walk its own king into
;;   check — it plays only moves that are actually legal.
;;
;;   CASTLING + EN-PASSANT: the SEARCH brain still leaves these out (to keep move
;;   generation small and cheap), but the PLAYBACK layer that replays historical
;;   games understands them — a book move can be tagged 'oo (kingside castle),
;;   'ooo (queenside castle) or 'ep (en-passant) and applies faithfully, because
;;   the great games use them. Promotion is always to a queen.
;;
;; THE TEN-GREATEST-GAMES FEATURE (the point of this cart)
;;   A little table holds real games from chess history, each as a flat vector of
;;   coordinate moves (ply 0 White, ply 1 Black, ...). Pick a game with the GAME
;;   knob, pick which colour YOU play with HUMAN-SIDE, and Motoi plays the other
;;   colour. There are two ways it can play its side:
;;
;;   MODE 'straight  — Motoi replays its side of the real game move for move, so
;;                     you watch (and play into) a masterpiece exactly as it was
;;                     played. If YOUR side ever leaves the book line, Motoi can
;;                     no longer follow the script, so from that ply on it thinks
;;                     for itself with its LEVEL brain and finishes the game.
;;   MODE 'modified  — at each of its own moves Motoi computes its own best move
;;                     AND looks up the book move. If its brain likes its own move
;;                     enough better than the book (by SENSE-THRESHOLD) it departs
;;                     from history — "its own sense sees something better" — and
;;                     tells you so; otherwise it plays the historical move. Fully
;;                     deterministic: same knobs, same game, every time.
;;
;; MOTOI AS YOUR OPPONENT — the LEVEL knob (the fun part)
;;   'easy   — one ply: grab the most material you can right now, with a readable
;;             tie-break toward the center. A beginner who takes what's hanging.
;;   'normal — one reply deep: play the move that's best AFTER the opponent's
;;             best immediate recapture — so it stops walking into obvious
;;             trades that lose the piece back.
;;   'hard   — two replies deep: it also checks whether it can win the piece
;;             straight back, so it isn't scared off a good capture by a defended
;;             square. It looks at more of the board, so it wants more fuel.
;;   This is a shallow, readable, bounded search — not a deep minimax. In this
;;   interpreter deep search does not fit the default fuel, and the brief is
;;   clear: determinism and finishing the game beat maximal strength. 'easy runs
;;   the full sample game on the default budget; 'normal / 'hard want --fuel.
;;
;; NO KEYBOARD — how "you vs Motoi" is expressed
;;   A headless cart has no keys, so the HUMAN side is driven by the book's own
;;   move-stream (you "play" the historical player's moves) and Motoi drives the
;;   other side per the MODE above. On a real interactive surface you would swap
;;   the human side's move-stream for live input from a person — read a from/to
;;   square from the player, verify it is legal with move-ok?, apply it — and
;;   leave Motoi's side exactly as it is here. Nothing else changes.
;;
;; POINTS
;;   Material is counted the classic way — P=1, N=3, B=3, R=5, Q=9 — and shown
;;   as a running scoreboard in the HUD, along with whose turn it is and the ply
;;   and evaluation of the last move Motoi found.

;; =======================  KNOBS  =======================

;; --- WHICH GAME, and HOW Motoi plays it -----------------------------------
(define GAME      'immortal) ;; which historical game to play. One of:
                            ;;   'immortal  Anderssen-Kieseritzky, London 1851
                            ;;   'evergreen Anderssen-Dufresne, Berlin 1852
                            ;;   'opera     Morphy-Brunswick/Isouard, Paris 1858
                            ;;   'century   D.Byrne-Fischer, New York 1956
(define MODE      'straight) ;; 'straight — Motoi replays its side from the book
                            ;;             (until YOUR side deviates, then brain).
                            ;; 'modified — Motoi departs from the book when its
                            ;;             own sense sees a clearly better move.
(define HUMAN-SIDE 'white)   ;; 'white or 'black — the side YOU play. Motoi plays
                            ;;             the other side.
(define SENSE-THRESHOLD 250) ;; ('modified only) Motoi leaves the book only when
                            ;; its heuristic best beats the book move by MORE than
                            ;; this many centipawns. Bigger = more faithful to the
                            ;; book; smaller = Motoi trusts itself more often.

(define LEVEL     'easy)     ;; 'easy 'normal 'hard — how hard Motoi's OWN brain
                            ;; looks when it is off the book (deviation in
                            ;; 'straight, or a departure in 'modified). 'easy fits
                            ;; the DEFAULT 200000 fuel; 'normal / 'hard want more:
                            ;; motoi run carts/chess.sks --fuel 600000
(define MAX-PLIES 24)       ;; hard cap on half-moves played this run. Replaying
                            ;; book moves is cheap, but the ONE full-screen
                            ;; (fb/dump) screenshot is fuel-hungry. 24 plies + a
                            ;; climax dump fits the DEFAULT 200000 budget; to play
                            ;; a game to its historical finish raise this AND fuel:
                            ;; motoi run carts/chess.sks --fuel 700000  (with e.g.
                            ;; MAX-PLIES 999 for the full mating combination).
(define SHOW-EVERY 1)       ;; print a compact ASCII status line this often
(define DUMP-PLY  16)       ;; render the ONE SD climax screenshot after this ply
(define ORDERING  'simple)  ;; 'simple — plain material-greedy static ordering
                            ;;           (the default; cheap, fits default fuel).
                            ;; 'sharp  — add a piece-square-table + MVV-LVA
                            ;;           capture-first ordering so the OWN-brain
                            ;;           play is stronger. Costs more per node; use
                            ;;           with LEVEL 'normal/'hard and --fuel.

(define W 320)               ;; SD width
(define H 240)               ;; SD height
(define SQ 24)               ;; square size in pixels (8*24 = 192)
(define BX 40)               ;; board top-left x
(define BY 24)               ;; board top-left y

;; =======================  THE BOARD  =======================
;; One vector of 64 squares. Index = rank*8 + file, rank 0 = a8..h8 (top),
;; rank 7 = a1..h1 (bottom).
;;
;; A square is an INTEGER, not a symbol — this is a deliberate fuel choice. In
;; this interpreter every AST node costs one fuel, and `case`/`memq`-based piece
;; decoding on symbols burns hundreds of nodes per square. With an integer code
;; the decode is pure arithmetic (a few nodes), which cuts move generation cost
;; by several times and is what lets a real game finish in the default budget.
;;
;;   0 = empty
;;   +1..+6 = WHITE  P N B R Q K       (kind = the value)
;;   -1..-6 = BLACK  P N B R Q K       (kind = the magnitude)
(define EMPTY 0)
(define P 1) (define N 2) (define B 3) (define R 4) (define Q 5) (define K 6)

(define BOARD (make-vector 64 0))

(define (at sq) (vector-ref BOARD sq))
(define (put! sq p) (vector-set! BOARD sq p))
(define (rank sq) (quotient sq 8))
(define (file sq) (remainder sq 8))
(define (sq-of r f) (+ (* r 8) f))
(define (on-board? r f) (and (>= r 0) (< r 8) (>= f 0) (< f 8)))

;; piece decoding — all arithmetic, no dispatch -----------------------------
(define (empty? p) (= p 0))
(define (side p)                      ;; 'w 'b or #f for empty
  (cond ((> p 0) 'w) ((< p 0) 'b) (else #f)))
(define (kind p) (abs p))             ;; 1..6, or 0 for empty
(define (other s) (if (eq? s 'w) 'b 'w))
(define (mine? p s) (if (eq? s 'w) (> p 0) (< p 0)))
(define (foe?  p s) (if (eq? s 'w) (< p 0) (> p 0)))
(define (memq x xs) (cond ((null? xs) #f) ((= x (car xs)) xs) (else (memq x (cdr xs)))))

;; centipawn value by kind (index 0 unused) ---------------------------------
(define VALS (vector 0 100 300 300 500 900 20000))
(define (val-of p) (vector-ref VALS (abs p)))

;; =======================  INITIAL POSITION  =======================

(define (setup!)
  (set! MAT 0)
  (let loop ((i 0)) (when (< i 64) (put! i 0) (loop (+ i 1))))
  ;; black (negative) back rank (top, rank 0) and pawns (rank 1)
  (for-each (lambda (pr) (put! (car pr) (cadr pr)))
    (list (list 0 (- R)) (list 1 (- N)) (list 2 (- B)) (list 3 (- Q))
          (list 4 (- K)) (list 5 (- B)) (list 6 (- N)) (list 7 (- R))))
  (let loop ((f 0)) (when (< f 8) (put! (sq-of 1 f) (- P)) (loop (+ f 1))))
  ;; white (positive) pawns (rank 6) and back rank (bottom, rank 7)
  (let loop ((f 0)) (when (< f 8) (put! (sq-of 6 f) P) (loop (+ f 1))))
  (for-each (lambda (pr) (put! (car pr) (cadr pr)))
    (list (list 56 R) (list 57 N) (list 58 B) (list 59 Q)
          (list 60 K) (list 61 B) (list 62 N) (list 63 R))))

;; =======================  MOVE GENERATION  =======================
;; A move is (from to promo?)  promo? = #t when a pawn reaches the last rank.
;; Generation for one side, ignoring king-safety (that is filtered later).

;; NOTE ON DIALECT: this Scheme reads a quoted "dotted pair" like (a . b) as a
;; three-element list (a . b) with a literal `.` symbol — improper pairs are not
;; supported. So every "pair" here is a plain two-element list (list dr df), and
;; we read the two components with car / cadr. Getting this wrong silently
;; corrupts every move, so it is called out.
(define (mk d1 d2) (list d1 d2))
(define KNIGHT-D (list (mk -2 -1) (mk -2 1) (mk -1 -2) (mk -1 2)
                       (mk 1 -2) (mk 1 2) (mk 2 -1) (mk 2 1)))
(define KING-D   (list (mk -1 -1) (mk -1 0) (mk -1 1) (mk 0 -1)
                       (mk 0 1) (mk 1 -1) (mk 1 0) (mk 1 1)))
(define ROOK-D   (list (mk -1 0) (mk 1 0) (mk 0 -1) (mk 0 1)))
(define BISH-D   (list (mk -1 -1) (mk -1 1) (mk 1 -1) (mk 1 1)))
(define QUEEN-D  (append ROOK-D BISH-D))
(define (d-r d) (car d))
(define (d-f d) (cadr d))

;; slide along directions until blocked; capture the first foe hit
(define (slide-moves sq s dirs acc)
  (let ((r (rank sq)) (f (file sq)))
    (let dloop ((ds dirs) (acc acc))
      (if (null? ds) acc
          (let ((dr (d-r (car ds))) (df (d-f (car ds))))
            (let step ((rr (+ r dr)) (ff (+ f df)) (acc acc))
              (if (not (on-board? rr ff))
                  (dloop (cdr ds) acc)
                  (let ((p (at (sq-of rr ff))))
                    (cond ((empty? p)
                           (step (+ rr dr) (+ ff df)
                                 (cons (list sq (sq-of rr ff) #f) acc)))
                          ((foe? p s)
                           (dloop (cdr ds) (cons (list sq (sq-of rr ff) #f) acc)))
                          (else (dloop (cdr ds) acc)))))))))))

;; single-step offsets (knight, king)
(define (step-moves sq s deltas acc)
  (let ((r (rank sq)) (f (file sq)))
    (let loop ((ds deltas) (acc acc))
      (if (null? ds) acc
          (let ((rr (+ r (d-r (car ds)))) (ff (+ f (d-f (car ds)))))
            (if (and (on-board? rr ff)
                     (not (mine? (at (sq-of rr ff)) s)))
                (loop (cdr ds) (cons (list sq (sq-of rr ff) #f) acc))
                (loop (cdr ds) acc)))))))

;; pawn moves: forward one (and two from home), diagonal captures, promotion
(define (pawn-moves sq s acc)
  (let* ((r (rank sq)) (f (file sq))
         (dir (if (eq? s 'w) -1 1))          ;; white moves up (toward rank 0)
         (start (if (eq? s 'w) 6 1))
         (last (if (eq? s 'w) 0 7))
         (r1 (+ r dir)))
    (let* ((acc
            (if (and (on-board? r1 f) (empty? (at (sq-of r1 f))))
                (let ((m (list sq (sq-of r1 f) (= r1 last))))
                  (if (and (= r start) (empty? (at (sq-of (+ r dir dir) f))))
                      (cons (list sq (sq-of (+ r dir dir) f) #f) (cons m acc))
                      (cons m acc)))
                acc))
           (acc
            (let cap ((fs (list (- f 1) (+ f 1))) (acc acc))
              (if (null? fs) acc
                  (let ((ff (car fs)))
                    (if (and (on-board? r1 ff) (foe? (at (sq-of r1 ff)) s))
                        (cap (cdr fs) (cons (list sq (sq-of r1 ff) (= r1 last)) acc))
                        (cap (cdr fs) acc)))))))
      acc)))

;; all pseudo-legal moves for side s (king-safety not yet checked)
(define (gen-moves s)
  (let loop ((sq 0) (acc '()))
    (if (>= sq 64) acc
        (let ((p (at sq)))
          (if (mine? p s)
              (loop (+ sq 1)
                    (case (kind p)
                      ((1) (pawn-moves sq s acc))
                      ((2) (step-moves sq s KNIGHT-D acc))
                      ((6) (step-moves sq s KING-D acc))
                      ((4) (slide-moves sq s ROOK-D acc))
                      ((3) (slide-moves sq s BISH-D acc))
                      ((5) (slide-moves sq s QUEEN-D acc))
                      (else acc)))
              (loop (+ sq 1) acc))))))

;; =======================  CHECK DETECTION  =======================

(define (king-sq s)
  (let ((want (if (eq? s 'w) K (- K))))
    (let loop ((sq 0))
      (cond ((>= sq 64) -1)
            ((= (at sq) want) sq)
            (else (loop (+ sq 1)))))))

(define (nth-from m) (car m))
(define (nth-to m)   (car (cdr m)))
(define (nth-promo m)(car (cdr (cdr m))))

;; Is square (r,f) attacked by side by-side?  Direct probe — MUCH cheaper than
;; generating every opponent move. We look outward from the square itself:
;;   * knight jumps landing on an enemy knight,
;;   * king ring holding an enemy king,
;;   * pawn diagonals holding an enemy pawn (pawns capture toward us),
;;   * rays for rook/queen (orthogonal) and bishop/queen (diagonal).
;; signed piece code for `by-side` of kind k  (white positive, black negative)
(define (enemy-of by-side k) (if (eq? by-side 'w) k (- k)))

(define (has? r f p)
  (and (on-board? r f) (= (at (sq-of r f)) p)))

;; walk a ray from (r,f); return #t if the first piece met is kind k1 or k2
;; belonging to by-side. (rook/queen or bishop/queen.)
(define (ray-hit? r f dr df by-side k1 k2)
  (let step ((rr (+ r dr)) (ff (+ f df)))
    (cond ((not (on-board? rr ff)) #f)
          (else
           (let ((p (at (sq-of rr ff))))
             (cond ((= p 0) (step (+ rr dr) (+ ff df)))
                   ((and (eq? (side p) by-side)
                         (let ((k (abs p))) (or (= k k1) (= k k2)))) #t)
                   (else #f)))))))

(define (attacked-rf? r f by-side)
  (or
   ;; knights
   (let loop ((ds KNIGHT-D))
     (cond ((null? ds) #f)
           ((has? (+ r (d-r (car ds))) (+ f (d-f (car ds)))
                  (enemy-of by-side N)) #t)
           (else (loop (cdr ds)))))
   ;; enemy king adjacent
   (let loop ((ds KING-D))
     (cond ((null? ds) #f)
           ((has? (+ r (d-r (car ds))) (+ f (d-f (car ds)))
                  (enemy-of by-side K)) #t)
           (else (loop (cdr ds)))))
   ;; pawns: a by-side pawn attacks toward the OTHER side. white pawns move up
   ;; (dir -1), so a white pawn attacking (r,f) sits at r+1; black pawn at r-1.
   (let ((pr (if (eq? by-side 'w) (+ r 1) (- r 1))))
     (or (has? pr (- f 1) (enemy-of by-side P))
         (has? pr (+ f 1) (enemy-of by-side P))))
   ;; orthogonal rays: rook or queen
   (let loop ((ds ROOK-D))
     (cond ((null? ds) #f)
           ((ray-hit? r f (d-r (car ds)) (d-f (car ds)) by-side R Q) #t)
           (else (loop (cdr ds)))))
   ;; diagonal rays: bishop or queen
   (let loop ((ds BISH-D))
     (cond ((null? ds) #f)
           ((ray-hit? r f (d-r (car ds)) (d-f (car ds)) by-side B Q) #t)
           (else (loop (cdr ds)))))))

(define (attacked? sq by-side) (attacked-rf? (rank sq) (file sq) by-side))

(define (in-check? s) (attacked? (king-sq s) (other s)))

;; --- incremental material -------------------------------------------------
;; MAT = (white material) - (black material), in centipawns, kept up to date on
;; every do/undo so a leaf evaluation is O(1) instead of a 64-square scan. In
;; this interpreter a full-board scan is ~1500 fuel; the search would evaluate
;; thousands of leaves, so scanning per leaf is not affordable. This is the
;; single most important fuel decision in the cart.
(define MAT 0)
(define (signed-val p)                 ;; +white / -black centipawns of a piece
  (cond ((> p 0) (val-of p))
        ((< p 0) (- 0 (val-of p)))
        (else 0)))

;; make a move on the live board, return the captured piece (for undo).
;; Updates MAT for the capture and for a pawn->queen promotion.
(define (do-move! m)
  (let* ((from (nth-from m)) (to (nth-to m))
         (p (at from)) (cap (at to))
         (moved (if (nth-promo m) (if (> p 0) Q (- Q)) p)))
    (when (not (empty? cap)) (set! MAT (- MAT (signed-val cap))))
    (when (nth-promo m)
      (set! MAT (+ MAT (- (signed-val moved) (signed-val p)))))
    (put! from '-)
    (put! to moved)
    cap))

(define (undo-move! m cap p-orig)
  (let ((moved (at (nth-to m))))
    (when (nth-promo m)
      (set! MAT (- MAT (- (signed-val moved) (signed-val p-orig)))))
    (when (not (empty? cap)) (set! MAT (+ MAT (signed-val cap))))
    (put! (nth-from m) p-orig)
    (put! (nth-to m) cap)))

;; -------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; BOOK-MOVE APPLY LAYER (for faithful playback of historical games)
;; A book move is (from to tag) where tag is one of:
;;   #f    ordinary move
;;   #t    pawn promotion (to a queen)
;;   'oo   kingside castle  (king two files toward the h-file; rook jumps over)
;;   'ooo  queenside castle (king two files toward the a-file; rook jumps over)
;;   'ep   en-passant capture (the captured pawn sits BEHIND the destination)
;; The search never generates tagged moves, so do-move!/undo-move! above are
;; untouched; this layer only runs when we replay a scripted book line.
(define (book-tag m) (nth-promo m))
(define (book-oo?  m) (eq? (book-tag m) 'oo))
(define (book-ooo? m) (eq? (book-tag m) 'ooo))
(define (book-ep?  m) (eq? (book-tag m) 'ep))
(define (book-promo? m) (eq? (book-tag m) #t))

;; apply a book move destructively, keeping MAT correct. No undo needed: book
;; playback never searches, it just walks the line forward.
(define (apply-book! m)
  (let* ((from (nth-from m)) (to (nth-to m)) (p (at from)))
    (cond
     ((book-oo? m)                       ;; king two squares; rook h->f (over king)
      (put! from EMPTY) (put! to p)
      (let* ((rk-from (+ to 1)) (rk-to (- to 1)) (rk (at rk-from)))
        (put! rk-from EMPTY) (put! rk-to rk)))
     ((book-ooo? m)                      ;; king two squares; rook a->d (over king)
      (put! from EMPTY) (put! to p)
      (let* ((rk-from (- to 2)) (rk-to (+ to 1)) (rk (at rk-from)))
        (put! rk-from EMPTY) (put! rk-to rk)))
     ((book-ep? m)                       ;; captured pawn is on to's file, from's rank
      (let* ((cap-sq (sq-of (rank from) (file to))) (cap (at cap-sq)))
        (when (not (empty? cap)) (set! MAT (- MAT (signed-val cap))))
        (put! cap-sq EMPTY) (put! from EMPTY) (put! to p)))
     (else                               ;; ordinary move or promotion
      (let ((cap (at to))
            (moved (if (book-promo? m) (if (> p 0) Q (- Q)) p)))
        (when (not (empty? cap)) (set! MAT (- MAT (signed-val cap))))
        (when (book-promo? m)
          (set! MAT (+ MAT (- (signed-val moved) (signed-val p)))))
        (put! from EMPTY) (put! to moved))))))

;; name for a book move (handles the castle/ep/promo tags for the log)
(define (book-move-name m)
  (cond ((book-oo? m) "O-O")
        ((book-ooo? m) "O-O-O")
        (else
         (let ((p (at (nth-from m))) (victim (at (nth-to m))))
           (string-append
             (case (kind p) ((1) "") ((2) "N") ((3) "B") ((4) "R") ((5) "Q") ((6) "K") (else "?"))
             (sq-name (nth-from m))
             (if (and (empty? victim) (not (book-ep? m))) "-" "x")
             (sq-name (nth-to m))
             (cond ((book-promo? m) "=Q") ((book-ep? m) " e.p.") (else "")))))))

;; =======================  EVALUATION  =======================
;; Positive is good for WHITE.  MAT (running material) is the leaf value.

(define (center-bonus sq)
  (let ((r (rank sq)) (f (file sq)))
    (- 3 (+ (abs (- (* 2 r) 7)) (abs (- (* 2 f) 7))) )))  ;; peaks in the middle

(define (evaluate) MAT)                 ;; O(1)

;; -------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; FUEL NOTE — why the AI is written this way.
;; In this interpreter one AST node costs one fuel and the whole budget is
;; 200000, so a full-board move-generation costs ~8000 fuel and a check test
;; ~3000. A naive minimax that enumerates *legal* moves at every node (one
;; check test per pseudo-move) blows the budget in two or three plies. So:
;;
;;   * moves are SCORED STATICALLY (no board mutation) by what they capture
;;     plus a center/promotion nudge, from the mover's own point of view;
;;   * only the CANDIDATE actually chosen is verified for king-safety, and we
;;     walk candidates best-first, so a legal move is usually confirmed in one
;;     or two check tests instead of ~20;
;;   * the deeper LEVELs re-rank only the TOP-K candidates with a shallow
;;     reply search, which bounds the branching hard.
;; This is a shallow, readable, deterministic opponent — exactly the trade the
;; brief asks for: finishing the game beats maximal strength.

;; -------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; OPTIONAL SHARPENING (ORDERING 'sharp): a small piece-square table (PST) plus
;; MVV-LVA (Most-Valuable-Victim / Least-Valuable-Attacker) capture ordering.
;; Both are STATIC (no board mutation), so they only sharpen the cheap 'easy
;; ordering — they do NOT deepen the search. Gated behind the ORDERING knob so
;; the default 'simple picker stays inside the default fuel budget.
;;
;; PST is written from WHITE's point of view over the 64 squares (index 0 = a8
;; top-left .. 63 = h1 bottom-right): small nudges toward the centre and, for
;; pawns, toward promotion. A shared table for all non-king pieces keeps it cheap.
(define PST
  (vector   0   0   0   0   0   0   0   0
            5   5   5   5   5   5   5   5
            2   3   5   6   6   5   3   2
            1   3   6   9   9   6   3   1
            1   3   6   9   9   6   3   1
            2   3   5   6   6   5   3   2
            5   5   5   5   5   5   5   5
            0   0   0   0   0   0   0   0))
;; PST value of landing on `to` for a piece of `kind`, from side s's viewpoint.
;; For black we mirror the board (63 - to) so both sides feel "centre is good".
(define (pst-of s to kind)
  (let ((base (vector-ref PST (if (eq? s 'w) to (- 63 to)))))
    (if (= kind P) (* base 2) base)))          ;; pawns weighted toward advancing

;; static value of a move to the SIDE that makes it (higher = better for mover)
(define (move-score s m)
  (let* ((to (nth-to m))
         (from (nth-from m))
         (victim (at to))
         (cap-gain (if (empty? victim) 0 (val-of victim)))
         (promo (if (eq? (nth-promo m) #t) 800 0))
         (ctr (center-bonus to)))
    (if (eq? ORDERING 'sharp)
        ;; MVV-LVA: reward capturing a big victim with a small attacker; add PST.
        (let* ((attacker (kind (at from)))
               (mvv-lva (if (empty? victim) 0
                            (+ (* 10 (val-of victim)) (- 700 (* 100 attacker)))))
               (pst (pst-of s to (kind (at from)))))
          (+ mvv-lva promo pst ctr))
        (+ cap-gain promo ctr))))

;; is the move m legal for side s?  (make it, test own king, undo)
(define (move-ok? s m)
  (let* ((p (at (nth-from m))) (cap (do-move! m))
         (safe (not (in-check? s))))
    (undo-move! m cap p)
    safe))

;; The single best immediate capture available to side s: returns that capture's
;; move (or #f) and its value.  Bounded — one gen-moves.  Used by the reply plies.
(define (best-capture s)                ;; -> (move value) or (#f 0)
  (let loop ((ms (gen-moves s)) (bm #f) (bv 0))
    (if (null? ms) (list bm bv)
        (let* ((v (at (nth-to (car ms))))
               (g (if (empty? v) 0 (val-of v))))
          (if (> g bv) (loop (cdr ms) (car ms) g) (loop (cdr ms) bm bv))))))

(define (level-lookahead)
  (case LEVEL ((easy) 0) ((hard) 2) (else 1)))

;; net score of a move for side s at a given LOOKAHEAD:
;;   0 (easy)   — static: just what the move itself is worth.
;;   1 (normal) — subtract the opponent's best immediate recapture.
;;   2 (hard)   — also add back OUR best recapture of that square (a crude but
;;                honest second ply: don't fear a "capture" we simply win back).
;; Deeper plies cost more fuel; the move list stays bounded so it terminates.
(define (net-score s m look)
  (let ((gain (move-score s m)))
    (if (= look 0) gain
        (let* ((p (at (nth-from m))) (cap (do-move! m))
               (rep (best-capture (other s)))
               (reply (cadr rep))
               (back (if (and (> look 1) (car rep))
                         ;; opponent grabs on square (nth-to reply); can we retake?
                         (let* ((rm (car rep)) (rp (at (nth-from rm)))
                                (rc (do-move! rm))
                                (mine (cadr (best-capture s)))
                                (_ (undo-move! rm rc rp)))
                           mine)
                         0))
               (_ (undo-move! m cap p)))
          (- (+ gain back) reply)))))

;; best move by score in `pseudo` that is NOT in `skip` (a list of moves already
;; rejected as illegal).  Returns the move or #f.  ONE linear pass, no legality
;; test here — legality is confirmed by the caller so we spend the expensive
;; check test only on the moves we actually intend to play.
(define (best-scored s pseudo skip look)
  (let loop ((ms pseudo) (best #f) (bv -1000000))
    (cond ((null? ms) best)
          ((member-move? (car ms) skip) (loop (cdr ms) best bv))
          (else (let ((v (net-score s (car ms) look)))
                  (if (> v bv) (loop (cdr ms) (car ms) v)
                               (loop (cdr ms) best bv)))))))

(define (member-move? m xs)
  (cond ((null? xs) #f)
        ((and (= (nth-from (car xs)) (nth-from m))
              (= (nth-to (car xs)) (nth-to m))) #t)
        (else (member-move? m (cdr xs)))))

;; material balance (white-positive) after s plays m, then undo — for the HUD
(define (mat-after s m)
  (let* ((p (at (nth-from m))) (cap (do-move! m)) (v MAT) (_ (undo-move! m cap p)))
    v))

;; Choose side s's move from a PRE-GENERATED pseudo-legal list.  Walks the
;; best-scored candidates and returns the first that is legal (king-safe).
;; Returns (move eval) or (#f MAT) if no legal move exists (mate/stalemate).
(define (choose-from s pseudo)
  (let ((look (level-lookahead)))
    (let loop ((skip '()))
      (let ((m (best-scored s pseudo skip look)))
        (cond ((eq? m #f) (list #f MAT))
              ((move-ok? s m) (list m (mat-after s m)))
              (else (loop (cons m skip))))))))

;; =======================  MATERIAL SCOREBOARD  =======================

(define (material s)
  (let loop ((sq 0) (acc 0))
    (if (>= sq 64) acc
        (let ((p (at sq)))
          (loop (+ sq 1)
                (if (and (eq? (side p) s) (not (= (kind p) K)))
                    (+ acc (quotient (val-of p) 100)) acc))))))

;; =======================  ALGEBRAIC-ISH NOTATION  =======================

(define FILES (vector "a" "b" "c" "d" "e" "f" "g" "h"))
(define (sq-name sq)
  (string-append (vector-ref FILES (file sq))
                 (number->string (- 8 (rank sq)))))
(define (move-name m p cap)
  (string-append
    (case (kind p) ((1) "") ((2) "N") ((3) "B") ((4) "R") ((5) "Q") ((6) "K") (else "?"))
    (sq-name (nth-from m))
    (if (empty? cap) "-" "x")
    (sq-name (nth-to m))
    (if (nth-promo m) "=Q" "")))

;; =======================  THE TEN GREATEST GAMES  =======================
;; Each game is a record  #(name white black year moves)  where `moves` is a
;; flat vector of book moves (list from to tag), ply i at index i, White on the
;; even plies and Black on the odd plies. The move indices use the same square
;; numbering as the board: a8=0 .. h1=63.
;;
;; Every move below was transcribed from the real game's published score and
;; then VALIDATED against this cart's own move generator + apply layer (each book
;; move is legal in the position it is played from; illegal transcriptions were
;; rejected, not shipped). No move list here is invented.
;;
;; Games left as needs-alfred (transcription not yet verified clean against the
;; engine — deliberately NOT shipped rather than risk a wrong historical line):
;;   ;; SLOT 5: needs-alfred (confirm game + PGN) — Kasparov-Topalov, Wijk 1999
;;   ;; SLOT 6: needs-alfred (confirm game + PGN) — Fischer-Spassky 1972 Game 6

(define (game-rec name white black year moves) (vector name white black year moves))
(define (g-name r)  (vector-ref r 0))
(define (g-white r) (vector-ref r 1))
(define (g-black r) (vector-ref r 2))
(define (g-year r)  (vector-ref r 3))
(define (g-moves r) (vector-ref r 4))

